        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Pachacuti</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1403 CE – c. 1471 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, at the palace of Cusicancha, in 1403 according to the historian José Antonio del Busto Duthurburu; given name Cusi Yupanqui, of the lineage Iñaca <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panakas">Panaka</a>, and according to the Spanish chroniclers the son of the eighth ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Inca Viracocha</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">1471 in the standard chronology, of a terminal illness; some colonial sources hint that he had abdicated before his death</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ninth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, reigning from 1438 to 1471 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-howland-rowe">John Howland Rowe</a>'s chronology (other scholars give 1400–1448, 1410–1450, 1420–1472 or 1425–1471); founder of the imperial lineage Hatun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">Ayllu</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> against the Chankas; transforming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">Chiefdom</a> of Cusco into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>; rebuilding Cusco and the sun temple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Qurikancha</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a>, believed to have been built as his estate; conquests around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>; forced resettlement of colonists (mitimaes); his link with the cult of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a></span></div>
        </div>
