    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> accounts recorded by Spanish chroniclers, the young Cusi Yupanqui defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> against the Chankas when the ruler fled, took power and renamed himself Pachacuti.</li><li>He and his generals began the conquests that within three generations carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule from the valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> across a sizeable part of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, while he rebuilt Cusco as an imperial city.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> did not count years, the sources are colonial and partisan, and scholars still disagree whether he was a historical emperor or a mythological figure for the start of Inca expansion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The familiar form Pachacútec was introduced by the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega">Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</a> in 1609 and is ungrammatical in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>. Documents of the 16th century write Pachacuti, reconstructed as pacha kutiy, &quot;the turn of the world&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

