    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1403</span><span class="rf-text">Cusi Yupanqui, the later Pachacuti, is born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> at the palace of Cusicancha, in the year given by the historian José Antonio del Busto Duthurburu</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1424</span><span class="rf-text">In del Busto's chronology he rallies the army and defends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> against the Chankas after the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Inca Viracocha</a> and his co-ruler Urco flee</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1425</span><span class="rf-text">After a short civil war in which Urco dies he is crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> and takes the name Pachacuti, in the same chronology</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1450</span><span class="rf-text">He names his son Amaru Yupanqui co-ruler, at about the date del Busto gives, and later replaces him with another son, Tupac Yupanqui</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1461</span><span class="rf-text">Tupac Yupanqui leads his first military campaign around this year according to del Busto; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-howland-rowe">John Howland Rowe</a> has him take military command in 1463</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1471</span><span class="rf-text">Pachacuti dies of a terminal illness in the standard chronology, and Tupac <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Yupanqui succeeds him without recorded dispute</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-capac.jpg" alt="Manco Capac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac">Manco Capac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Manco_Capac,_First_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pachacuti's given name was Cusi Yupanqui, or Ripac. He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, at the palace of Cusicancha beside the Inticancha temple, into the lineage of Iñaca <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panakas">Panaka</a> in the moiety of Hanan Cusco, "high Cusco". According to the Spanish chroniclers he was the son of the eighth ruler of Cusco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Inca Viracocha</a>, and he is traditionally considered the son of Viracocha and Mama Runtu. The historian María Rostworowski concluded from the colonial writings that he was not Viracocha's son: Andean custom let the "most able" take power, which the Spaniards, who thought in terms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, read as illegitimacy. Catherine Julien holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> legitimacy rested on a concentration of "capac" status, inherited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac">Manco Capac</a> through both the female and the male line.</p><p>The Incas of Cusco did not systematically count years, so every date in his life is an approximation from colonial documents or archaeology. The historian José Antonio del Busto Duthurburu placed his birth in 1403. His tutor, Micuymana, is said to have taught him history, laws and language and the handling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipus</a>, the knotted cords used for records. In the traditions the chroniclers collected, Inca nobles admired him from a young age for the courage, intelligence and maturity that his brother Inca Urco, the appointed co-ruler and heir, lacked, and Viracocha's generals began to conspire to replace Urco. The main sources for his life are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-betanzos">Juan de Betanzos</a> and Pedro Sarmiento de Gamboa, both drawing on the "life history" kept by his descent group.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca.jpg" alt="Viracocha Inca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Viracocha Inca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Viracocha,_Eighth_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings_-_overall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early 15th century the Chankas, described as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>' traditional enemies, invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> confederation. Several versions of the encounter exist; in the one most accepted, the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Inca Viracocha</a> and his co-ruler Urco fled, while Cusi Yupanqui rallied the army with four of Viracocha's generals and prepared the city's defence. The Chankas were repelled so severely that, legend tells, even the stones rose up to fight on his side, and at the battle of Yahuar Pampa the Inca army won a decisive victory. Cusi Yupanqui brought captured Chanka leaders to his father to tread upon, a traditional victory ritual. Viracocha answered that the honour belonged to the designated heir, Urco. A heated argument followed, and Viracocha tried to have him assassinated; the plot failed.</p><p>Viracocha went into exile, and Inca Yupanqui returned in triumph to Cusco. After a short civil war in which Urco died he was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> and took the name Pachacuti, from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> concept meaning "the turn of the world", a cataclysmic change of eras. The ethnic groups around Cusco that had helped him received the status of "hawa inka", Incas by privilege. An exact date for the war is not known. Del Busto put the defence of Cusco in 1424 and the reign from 1425; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-howland-rowe">John Howland Rowe</a>'s standard chronology of 1945 begins the reign in 1438, though radiocarbon dates suggest the beginning of the 15th century. Since the early 1980s the academic consensus, following Pierre Duviols, has treated the Chanka attack as largely mythical; Terence N. D'Altroy allows that the saga may contain historical elements.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="Machu Picchu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machu_Picchu,_2023_(012).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Draceane, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Despite his prestige Pachacuti at first had "little effective power and a meager work force". He summoned the surrounding chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">kurakas</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> for lavish feasts and gifts, including the booty of the Chanka war, and then made growing demands under the Andean principle of reciprocity: warehouses, stocks of produce, an army, better roads. By these means he rebuilt much of Cusco as an imperial city, with a sector for each of the four suyus, and rebuilt the great sun temple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Qurikancha</a>. He is often linked to the origin and spread of the cult of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, and according to Garcilaso de la Vega he created the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti-raymi">Inti Raymi</a>. He conquered lands along the Urubamba valley, where most archaeologists believe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> was built as his estate. He built irrigation networks, terraces, roads and hospices.</p><p>His first campaign, led in person with his general Apo Mayta, struck the Chankas' former allies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> around Cusco. He then defeated the Colla chiefdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> at Ayaviri, capturing its ruler, occupied Hatunqulla and received the submission of the Lupacas, Pacasas and Azangaros. Afterwards he left command to his generals. Capac Yupanqui won the submission of Chincha on the coast and marched north as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>; Pachacuti's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Topa Inca Yupanqui</a> forced the Chimú ruler Minchançaman to surrender by cutting the irrigation canals that fed his capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>, and campaigned in what is now Ecuador. Scholars differ on how much of this was lasting conquest: Rowe judged the Chincha and Cajamarca campaigns to have been raids.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui.jpg" alt="Túpac Inca Yupanqui" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tupac-inca-yupanqui-small.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pachacuti's rule began in conflict with his own house: his father Viracocha tried to have him killed and his brother Urco died in the civil war that brought him to power. He spent the first months of his reign putting down revolts by chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> valley. While an expedition was in the eastern lowlands, the Colla, Lupaca and Azangaro rose under a son of the former Colla ruler; according to Sarmiento de Gamboa an army of around 200,000 men was assembled to put the revolt down. The accounts also record that he had two of his brothers, the general Capac Yupanqui and Huayna Yupanqui, killed after the northern campaign, and that he killed his sons Tilca Yupanqui and Auqui Yupanqui.</p><p>He began the practice of forced migration, sending colonists called mitimaes from loyal areas to unstable provinces, and is credited with displacing hundreds of thousands of people to the remote edges of the empire; these colonists held the lowest place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> social order. The record itself is contested. Sarmiento's history, commissioned by the Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, was written partly to justify Spanish rule, and Betanzos altered the story to place his wife's ancestor in the succession. Various historians associate Pachacuti with rewriting the reigns of earlier rulers to justify expansion. Pierre Duviols, Reiner Tom Zuidema, Juan Ossio Acuña, Gary Urton and Franck Garcia regard him as mythological or mytho-historical; Zuidema wrote that the rulers before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Topa Inca Yupanqui</a> were social groups or factions. Rowe, Rostworowski, Julien, Martti Pärssinen and others consider him historical.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Having conquered the nations around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in person, Pachacuti withdrew from campaigning to concentrate on administrative reforms and the embellishment of the city. Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> custom he named his crown prince, Amaru Yupanqui, co-ruler, to test his capacities and to avoid a war of succession. The traditions describe Amaru as a gentle man given to agriculture and the building of canals, "too human to be ruler". After 5 to 6 or 10 years, finding him without military talent, Pachacuti presented another son, Tupac Yupanqui, to the Inca nobles, who elected him co-ruler; other sources say Amaru's reign ended on his mother's death, because the nobility disdained him. Rostworowski suggested a reign of around 60 years in all: 40 alone, 5 to 6 with Amaru and 14 to 15 with Tupac Yupanqui.</p><p>In his last years government may in practice have lain with a "helper" or "brother" named Yamqui Yupanqui, who at Pachacuti's death confirmed Tupac Inca Yupanqui as successor. Pachacuti died in 1471, in the standard chronology, of a terminal illness, and his son succeeded without any recorded dispute. Some colonial sources hint that he had abdicated before his death. Some historians place a brief independent reign of Amaru Yupanqui after his death, ended by Tupac Yupanqui. Sarmiento de Gamboa attributed a song to him on his deathbed: "I was born as a lily in the garden, and like the lily I grew, as my age advanced / I became old and had to die, and so I withered and died." His mummy was taken at his own wish to the palace of Patallacta and was later found at Tococache.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/quipu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quipu.jpg" alt="Quipu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/quipu">Quipu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inca_Quipu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lyndsaruell at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pachacuti began an era of conquest that within three generations expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule from the valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> to a sizeable part of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. He did not improve the system of succession: in later generations each new Inca had to win enough support from the nobles, the priesthood and the army to prevail in a civil war or deter rivals. His deeds were handed down in genealogical histories, life histories and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipus</a> kept near his royal mummy by his lineage, Hatun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">Ayllu</a>; his life history began with the Chanka attack and ended with his death and the short poem attributed to him. He is also remembered as a poet, the author of the Sacred Hymns of the Situa.</p><p>In Andean cosmology he stands beside the creator deity Viracocha and the mythical first Inca, Manco Cápac, as the archetype of the perfect Inca ruler, who spread the Inca cultural model and pantheon among the peoples of the Andes. Modern scholarship is divided over what lies behind that figure. Rowe, comparing the colonial sources, argued for a state-sanctioned "standard history" in which the victory over the Chankas caused the imperial expansion, and Rostworowski's biography of 1953 supported him. Zuidema and Duviols concluded that the Inca state was a diarchy in which Pachacuti had a co-ruler, and Pärssinen that Cusco had three rulers. Catherine Julien found in 2009 that Zuidema's structuralism reorients the search for a meaningful Inca history but takes no account of change. Pachacuti appears today as the leader of the Incas in video games, novels and television series.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pachacuti <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Yupanqui, ninth ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in the 15th century, is credited with defeating the Chankas, rebuilding Cusco and beginning the conquests that made the Inca Empire. His story reaches us through colonial chroniclers, and scholars disagree on its dates and on how far it is history or myth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> is thought to have been his estate.</em></p>

